INT. COUNTY JAIL - ATTORNEY VISITING ROOM - AFTERNOON

A grey cinderblock cell, steel table bolted to the floor, two plastic chairs. A one-way window in the far wall. A fluorescent light buzzes.

CORA WINSLOW-ABBOTT (30s, defence attorney, rolled sleeves, legal pad) sits with a pen clicking. Across from her, in an orange jumpsuit, sits LILLIAN "LILY" MARSTON (30s, accountant, cuffed hands, composed face). Neither has blinked in a while.

CORA
Lily. Start at the beginning.

LILY
I'd rather start in the middle.

CORA
That's not how it works.

LILY
It's how I work.

Cora sets her pen down. Folds her hands.

CORA
Where were you at nine on the night of the fourteenth?

LILY
At the office.

CORA
Alone?

LILY
With Brenda. She was in the next room.

CORA
Brenda says she wasn't.

LILY
Brenda says a lot of things.

CORA
(to the window)
For the record, the client is being difficult.

LILY
I'm not difficult. I'm precise.

CORA
You were precise when you moved four million dollars.

LILY
I was precise when I moved it back.

CORA
Back?

LILY
She took it first.

CORA
Who did?

LILY
Brenda.

CORA
She says you did.

LILY
She lies.

CORA
(muttering)
They both do.

LILY
What was that?

CORA
Nothing. Notes to myself.

She scrawls on the legal pad. Lily watches the pen.

LILY
You think I'm guilty.

CORA
I think you're interesting.

LILY
That's not an answer.

CORA
It's the only honest one I have.

LILY
Fine.

She leans back. The chain between her wrists clinks against the table. Cora meets her eyes.

CORA
Here's what I know. She says you stole it. You say she stole it. One of you is right.

LILY
One of us is.

CORA
Or neither.

LILY
Then who?

CORA
That's what I intend to find out.

She stands, gathers the pad. Lily looks at her hands.

LILY
Cora.

CORA
Yes?

LILY
She took the accounts at eight-fifteen. I have the logs.

CORA
Where?

LILY
In my head.

Cora stops with her hand on the door.

CORA
(to herself)
Of course.
